00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:38.640
- So the topic of the talk
today is bias in algorithms

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:43.113
and my plan is to give the listeners

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:49.620
a sense of how in this very
objective world of algorithms,

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.533
which live in this very
precise mathematical framework,

00:05:56.050 --> 00:06:00.500
human-like qualities like bias can emerge,

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:04.780
and what can we do to intervene

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:07.810
and mitigate these algorithmic biases,

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:12.070
and in the process of trying to figure out

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:16.530
the ways in which we can
mitigate these biases

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:19.790
in the world of algorithms,
you will also see at the end,

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:24.413
how we can gain some insights
about mitigating bias

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:29.010
in the world of humans itself.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:34.010
So let me start by
setting up the framework

00:06:34.484 --> 00:06:36.390
the world in which we live in.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:40.000
So it's not news to anybody here

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:41.980
that humans live in an algorithmic world.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:44.130
Let me make this a
little bit more precise.

00:06:46.367 --> 00:06:51.367
Us as a society are consuming
a huge amount of information

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.790
through various online channels

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:59.030
unlike, you know, a few years ago

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.730
where we were looking
at newspapers and so on.

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:05.020
Now everybody gets their news
through Twitter, Facebook,

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:06.333
or equivalent.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:12.660
We connect with people through
these social applications,

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:16.330
which are made possible
through this worldwide web

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:17.163
or internet.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:23.430
We are also increasingly using algorithms

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:26.833
in the context of
governance, policy and so on.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:31.690
And last but not the least,
many of the utilities

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:35.660
that we consume today
from ride sharing apps

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:40.330
to online shopping
platforms are all governed

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.800
by decision-making algorithms,

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.020
which are constantly learning
about human preferences

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.433
through the data that they generate,

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:54.340
and presenting information
often in a personalized manner

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:57.000
to these individuals.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:01.920
And this information is affecting
who we are as individuals

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:06.557
and giving shape to the
society in a very different way

00:08:06.557 --> 00:08:10.180
and at a very different scale
than we imagined previously.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:14.053
So this is the context that
I'm going to be talking about.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:19.530
And let me say a few
words about algorithms.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:22.073
And I'm gonna focus on
artificial algorithms,

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:26.057
which largely developed, you know,

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:29.247
the formal study of algorithms

00:08:29.247 --> 00:08:31.800
and the discipline of
computer science is about,

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:35.280
you know, I would say a bit
less than a hundred years old

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:38.525
start with the works of Turing and others

00:08:38.525 --> 00:08:41.880
who started looking at algorithms

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:44.610
or formalizing the notion of algorithm.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:47.380
And in the last few decades,

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:49.510
we have come a very long way

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:52.693
in designing artificial algorithms,

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:55.580
which have shown the ability to mimic

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:58.870
some of the most complex
natural algorithms.

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:01.680
For instance, Priya had
mentioned protein folding.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:03.720
I mean, one of the most recent successes

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:07.060
of artificial algorithms
is the ability to predict

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:08.943
the tertiary structure of proteins,

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:12.910
which has been one of the main challenges.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:16.970
I have to mention beating the best players

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:19.770
in the most complex games,
predicting weather, you know,

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:25.280
and I guess the more relevant algorithms

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:28.410
that we are going to talk
about a little bit today

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.930
are things like, you know,
algorithms use in courts

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:34.850
and my judges which decide,
you know, who gets bail

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:36.350
and who doesn't get bail,

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:40.730
or artificial algorithms,

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:43.650
which are deciding who gets loan

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:45.800
and who doesn't get loan and so on.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:50.800
So there is a vast and complex
landscape of algorithms.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:56.950
Very, very, roughly you can think about

00:09:56.950 --> 00:10:01.670
an artificial algorithm in today's world

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:05.770
as something which has a few components,

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:07.060
not necessarily all of them,

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:10.280
but there may be some data available

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:13.623
from which the algorithm
might want to learn.

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:19.360
To learn something, we
need to have a model,

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:24.020
we need to figure out,
you know, what is allowed

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:25.150
for us to learn.

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:28.800
I mean, when we trying to learn from data,

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:31.220
what are we allowed to use
in that learning process?

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:32.660
And that's the step that I'm calling

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:34.760
the statistical or reasoning model.

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:37.830
And finally, once you
have decided on the model

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:42.830
and you have the data, you
will need an algorithm,

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:46.650
which can then find the
best model from this data.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:49.040
And we gonna talk a little
bit more about in detail.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.900
So this is just a very high level view

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:53.170
for you to get a sense

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:57.930
of what many of these
algorithms look like.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:01.410
And the point that I would
like to highlight is that

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:05.360
these decisions that we see here,

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:08.270
right from data to selection of models,

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:09.787
selection of training algorithm,

00:11:09.787 --> 00:11:13.060
the selection of anything involves humans.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:16.990
And so this is why we call it,

00:11:16.990 --> 00:11:19.260
I mean, artificial
intelligence algorithms,

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:22.590
because, you know, humans
are making these decisions.

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:24.230
One thing that remains common

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:28.010
no matter which artificial
algorithm you look at

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:32.050
is that they live in a very precise world,

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:36.373
which is governed by
unambiguous mathematical rules.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:42.600
And, you know, somehow people in society

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:46.220
associates objectivity to mathematics.

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:50.680
And we can go into that
at some other point,

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:52.651
but there is some association of objective

00:11:52.651 --> 00:11:56.380
to the mathematical precision.

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:59.260
And so there was some
expectation from these algorithms

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:02.020
that they should also be objective

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:04.883
and not show human-like qualities.

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:11.080
Unfortunately, this is not true

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:13.260
and I guess this is not
news to anybody here.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:16.403
Nevertheless, I will still
go through some examples.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:23.673
I would say about less than a decade ago

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:31.210
many researchers started
auditing algorithms,

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:34.830
which are being used in
very, very broad settings.

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.540
For instance, search.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:38.890
So you go on something like Google

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:40.760
and search for Google images

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:43.923
and search for something like a CEO.

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:46.720
And, you know, it's not like Google

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:49.600
just has these 15 images of CEO.

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:52.770
They got billions of images
probably which are not CEO.

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:56.180
And then based on what
it understands of you

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:58.340
and the word CEO,

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:03.340
it'll pull out a small subset
of images for you to see.

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:09.220
I mean, why small?

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:11.320
Because your screen is small,
your attention is small.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:13.593
So you can't really
process billion images,

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:16.840
and perhaps that's not
what you're looking for.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:21.840
So this process requires
solving an algorithmic problem

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:27.720
of selecting a subset for a
particular individual to view.

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:30.810
And presumably there are some objectives.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.960
I mean, you know, the search is for free.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:36.560
So there is clearly some
objective of showing

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:38.370
these images to you.

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.960
And one reasonable objective

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:46.960
that many of these companies
have is maximize engagement.

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:50.320
So we keep giving you good results

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:52.660
so that you keep spending
time on our platform

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:54.970
so that we can keep showing you ads,

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:57.533
and so that you can go
buy products, right?

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:03.950
More strikingly, and I mean, you know,

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:05.770
I guess less strikingly

00:14:05.770 --> 00:14:07.713
and more strikingly at the same time,

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:14.260
so facial recognition has been, you know,

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:18.150
very important in this whole
fight against algorithmic bias

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:21.620
and there are interesting
and important work

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:23.770
of Joy Buolamwini and Timnit Gebru

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:27.380
showed that off-the-shelf classifiers

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:30.490
manufactured by some of the most standard

00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:33.360
and well-trusted companies such as IBM

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:37.950
showed remarkable
differences in the ability

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:42.950
to classify people based on
their protected attributes,

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:44.633
such as race and gender.

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:49.890
And since I talked about
showing advertisements,

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:52.190
you know, showing advertisement
is in the core business

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:54.320
of many of these platforms.

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:57.460
And there also, there were biases.

00:14:57.460 --> 00:14:58.950
I mean, it's not surprising,

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:02.783
but it was observed
that women get shown ads

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:07.950
for low-paying jobs while
men get shown more ads

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:09.023
for high-paying jobs.

00:15:11.707 --> 00:15:13.950
Okay, so, you know, as somebody

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:16.220
who might have not seen this before,

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:17.087
or who's skeptic say,

00:15:17.087 --> 00:15:19.230
"Okay, you know, this
is what is being shown.

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:21.140
What is the problem with this?

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:26.140
You know, anybody can show
whatever they want to you.

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:27.630
So what is the issue?"

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:31.550
The issue is that the output
of the same algorithms

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:33.823
can alter human perceptions.

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:39.060
Okay, so it's not like just
seeing has no impact on us.

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:41.300
Seeing is believing in this case.

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:46.300
And the same studies,
which found these biases

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:51.850
also found that people

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:55.590
who may or may not have an
opinion about gender bias

00:15:55.590 --> 00:16:00.490
or gender and CEO's
after being shown images,

00:16:00.490 --> 00:16:04.590
which are distorted or
representation which are distorted,

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:07.933
have distorted perceptions
of what it means to be a CEO.

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:15.210
And I guess we have all
gone through this phase

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:17.000
and no matter which country you live in,

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:20.690
where we are seeing increasing distortion

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:22.110
or increasing divergence

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:24.390
without saying what is
right, what is wrong,

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:27.640
our worldviews are
diverging or polarizing.

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:32.640
So polarization has had a
disruptive effect on democracy.

00:16:34.110 --> 00:16:35.240
It has given people

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:38.140
the ability to manipulate
elections and so on.

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:41.120
So there is a lot of
stuff read about here,

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:45.470
and these are the core issues

00:16:45.470 --> 00:16:48.443
that I'm going to address in this talk.

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:52.610
But even before I go there,

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:56.720
I want to first address
the question of anatomy,

00:16:56.720 --> 00:16:59.860
how does bias emerge?

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:01.253
And to understand that,

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:05.770
we have to understand a
little bit in more detail

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:10.770
what I showed in my first
slide, how do we learn?

00:17:13.521 --> 00:17:16.520
You know, what is going
on in this algorithms?

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:19.030
So this is not a mathematical talk.

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:21.250
So unfortunately I will
not be able to show you

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:24.263
a lot of equations, in
other venues I would.

00:17:26.570 --> 00:17:31.440
But hopefully I will give
you some concrete idea,

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:34.100
more concrete than in the first slide

00:17:34.100 --> 00:17:36.489
so that, you know, you go
back with a very graphic sense

00:17:36.489 --> 00:17:40.003
of how these algorithms operate.

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:43.350
So the starting point is
the real world, right?

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:45.600
So this is the world in which we live in.

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:49.170
And when we talk about data,

00:17:49.170 --> 00:17:50.720
data doesn't live in the real world,

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:53.100
data is collected from the real world.

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:56.070
And to collect data, you
first need to figure out

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:57.560
how to represent data.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:00.673
So I'm a person.

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:05.710
If some organization, let's
say the U.S. government

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:07.880
wants to collect data from me,

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:12.880
they will specify that we need
your first name, last name,

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:16.660
age, this, you know, social, whatever.

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:21.600
They'll give me a template
and in most situations

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:22.790
I really do not have an option.

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:26.080
So I need to fill out all
this data and give it to them.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:31.080
So two points here; one is representation,

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:34.210
which is that in most cases,

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:36.570
I would be given some kind of a form.

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:39.040
It could be electronic, it
could be a physical form

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:41.520
where I fill out information.

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:43.580
It could be somebody
taking pictures, right?

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:45.010
So I need to add a picture,

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:49.530
which is also very specifically
specified in certain cases.

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:54.530
And then certain individuals are selected

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:56.520
for such data collection.

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:58.680
So if you are doing that survey

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:01.830
or trying to estimate
something about a population

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:06.080
is infeasible to collect
data from everybody.

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:09.600
So you will select some sub sample.

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:14.317
Now, to what extent the sample
is good is often not clear.

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:21.110
So data has these two major components.

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:23.210
There are other bells and whistles,

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:25.970
but in the first order
understanding of things,

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:27.620
this is what it is.

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:31.080
Now that you have a collection of data,

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:33.220
you want to learn from this data.

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:38.210
And learning happens
in a hypothesis class.

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:41.170
So the more complex the hypothesis class,

00:19:41.170 --> 00:19:45.240
the more you have the ability to learn.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:49.373
And most learning algorithms
will fix the hypothesis class.

00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:59.493
I mean these hypotheses are parameterized.

00:20:03.910 --> 00:20:05.170
I mean, it will not be the case

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:08.890
that one hypothesis will explain the data.

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:10.820
So there may be many, many hypothesis,

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:12.143
which explain the data.

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:15.953
So then comes the question
of which one to choose.

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:21.110
So which is the best hypothesis
that will explain the data?

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:24.040
So we have to fix a
mathematical notion of accuracy

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:27.360
or something which helps us select

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:29.550
from this wide class of hypothesis

00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:31.830
the one which is the best.

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:33.830
Once you have made these choices,

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:36.610
we go on to find the most accurate model,

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:41.363
and I'm using something
called the training algorithm.

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:47.340
And that gives rise to the
decision-making algorithm,

00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:52.050
which then is used in the real world.

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:54.250
So maybe this algorithm
will be used to decide

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:56.300
who to give loan or who not to give loan.

00:20:57.220 --> 00:20:59.300
And that has a direct social impact.

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:01.040
Wherever gets loan, their life changes

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:05.504
and whoever doesn't get loan,
their life probably worsens.

00:21:05.504 --> 00:21:08.130
I mean, given the fact that
they're looking for loan.

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:09.860
So this creates this feedback loop.

00:21:09.860 --> 00:21:11.630
I mean, output of these algorithms

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:13.400
could also feed into other algorithms

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:14.803
and so on and so forth.

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:20.510
So the design choices are human choices.

00:21:20.510 --> 00:21:22.350
They are driven by human motivations.

00:21:22.350 --> 00:21:24.290
So each step of this process,

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:27.010
there are humans who are deciding,

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:29.370
and their decisions are motivated

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:32.323
by what they think is valuable.

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:36.010
So the real question is,
you know, who decides?

00:21:37.555 --> 00:21:40.510
Who does this benefit and who is harmed?

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:42.400
And all of these questions
are very important.

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:45.950
I mean, these questions
are generally not asked

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:47.813
in the design of algorithms,

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:51.080
because whoever is
designing the algorithm,

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:55.280
it's their benefits that
certainly are incorporated

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:59.000
very, very rarely there a discussion

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:00.550
on who could be potentially harmed

00:22:00.550 --> 00:22:02.700
and what can we do to
mitigate those harms.

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:07.660
So in all of this algorithms
end up being brittle

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:12.253
and gain the ability
to encode human biases.

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:27.002
Well, one more point I
wanted to make is that,

00:22:27.002 --> 00:22:28.570
you know, one of the
reasons why this study

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:33.430
on algorithmic bias really
caught the attention of media

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:36.630
I mean, that's really where it got into

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:39.150
is because in the end, what does it show?

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:42.430
It shows and it gives definitive proof

00:22:42.430 --> 00:22:43.593
that humans are biased.

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:47.357
If I catch somebody and
say, "Hey, you're biased,"

00:22:49.026 --> 00:22:50.837
they may argue and wriggle
out their, you know, way

00:22:50.837 --> 00:22:52.590
through that discussion.

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:55.660
And in the end nothing
conclusive came out.

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:58.070
But if the same person
hands me an algorithm,

00:22:58.070 --> 00:23:00.760
which I can keep
repeating again and again,

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:02.180
I mean, keep running it again and again

00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:03.750
without any further intervention,

00:23:03.750 --> 00:23:06.390
once the algorithm is there, that's it.

00:23:06.390 --> 00:23:07.360
And then you can see,

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:08.710
look, I mean, if you're not biased

00:23:08.710 --> 00:23:12.400
and how can this algorithm be biased?

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:14.240
So it gives us a definitive proof.

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:15.610
Now without pointing fingers,

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:16.900
it gives us a definite proof

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:20.050
that humans are extremely biased,

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:23.210
and that's what these
algorithms are picking up.

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:27.023
So it gave us a lens
into our own existence.

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:32.860
And it should not be
surprising that whilst studying

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:36.470
or as a bigger goal of
studying algorithm bias

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:39.130
is to try to understand various ways

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:40.580
in which humans can be biased

00:23:40.580 --> 00:23:45.470
and design frameworks and
policies to mitigate those biases,

00:23:45.470 --> 00:23:47.843
and I'll come to that
towards the end of the talk.

00:23:50.704 --> 00:23:55.704
So just a little bit more
graphic image of what goes on.

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:00.820
If I take a simple example,

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:05.630
let's say of who to give loan or not,

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:07.842
let's say the society
consists of, you know,

00:24:07.842 --> 00:24:10.503
this is a very toy
caricaturization of the problem,

00:24:10.503 --> 00:24:12.060
this is not how it works,

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:16.820
but it'll give you a mathematical
sense of what goes on.

00:24:16.820 --> 00:24:21.170
So some people let's say are
women, and some people are men.

00:24:21.170 --> 00:24:23.090
I understand that things are not binary,

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:28.090
but for this thing,
let's just consider that.

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:31.190
And traditionally,
there has been disparity

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:32.890
because of the construction

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:35.183
of various social and political systems,

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:41.423
men and women in our society
are not equal, unfortunately.

00:24:42.790 --> 00:24:45.850
And suppose we choose features
corresponding to women,

00:24:45.850 --> 00:24:47.603
so suppose I pick two features,

00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:51.090
features, meaning what
about the individual

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:54.720
do I need to decide whether
to give someone a loan or not?

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:56.570
So let's say as a bank manager I decide,

00:24:56.570 --> 00:24:58.410
I'm going to ask to encode

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:01.430
to the extent to which
the person is educated

00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:03.130
and the income.

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:05.500
So let's say these are the two
coordinates here that you see

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:07.880
two numbers associated to each person.

00:25:07.880 --> 00:25:09.330
Some mathematical encoding

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:12.550
of their highest educational
degree in this case it's five

00:25:12.550 --> 00:25:15.863
and some mathematical
encoding in this case it's 10.

00:25:19.460 --> 00:25:22.190
This could be 10,000,
this could be 10 million,

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:25.720
but yeah, without assigning units to it,

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:27.803
I just think of the abstract.

00:25:29.210 --> 00:25:31.530
And then many of these
algorithms also need

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:36.370
some kind of, you know, historic data.

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:41.143
So in the historic data, I
would probably also need to know

00:25:41.143 --> 00:25:43.820
whether this kind of a person

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:45.840
ended up repaying their loan or not.

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:48.430
This information is useful for me,

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:49.670
but I don't want to give loans

00:25:49.670 --> 00:25:52.120
to people who are not
going to pay it back to me.

00:25:54.220 --> 00:25:58.620
And then, you know, I
talked a lot about models.

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:01.910
So models could be as
simple as selecting lines,

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:05.710
straight lines to divide
the data into positive

00:26:05.710 --> 00:26:06.913
and negative examples.

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:10.190
So plus means positive examples

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:12.363
and minus means negative examples,

00:26:15.009 --> 00:26:16.730
or it could be something more.

00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:18.570
It could be something very complicated.

00:26:18.570 --> 00:26:21.810
For example, you could
choose very complex curves

00:26:21.810 --> 00:26:22.910
in high dimensions,

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:25.933
which can kind of cherry
pick the points, if you like.

00:26:27.370 --> 00:26:30.100
So this is the traditional
machine learning algorithm,

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:34.230
and, I mean, these kinds of
images are more associated

00:26:34.230 --> 00:26:37.290
to deep learning algorithms
where there are many parameters,

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:39.083
very high degree polynomials,

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:44.120
and they can really
learn arbitrary concepts.

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:46.340
So then the nice thing
about those learning models

00:26:46.340 --> 00:26:47.720
is that they are universal

00:26:47.720 --> 00:26:50.020
in the sense that they
can learn any function.

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:54.903
So in all of these choices,

00:26:55.930 --> 00:26:58.760
and this is just a subset of the choices,

00:26:58.760 --> 00:26:59.953
there can be issues.

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:04.210
We already talked about
societal and historic biases.

00:27:05.710 --> 00:27:06.543
What may happen?

00:27:06.543 --> 00:27:08.450
You may say, "Oh, I'm not
using gender of a person.

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:09.283
So what's the problem?

00:27:09.283 --> 00:27:10.250
I mean, these numbers are fair."

00:27:10.250 --> 00:27:11.420
No, these numbers may not be fair

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:12.970
because, you know, as I said,

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:15.580
traditionally, women might
have lesser education,

00:27:15.580 --> 00:27:16.413
lesser income.

00:27:16.413 --> 00:27:20.707
So these numbers might be
tilted against them, right?

00:27:21.830 --> 00:27:23.550
And same about this, right?

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:27.740
I mean, the labels and other,

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:29.390
there can be many inaccuracies,

00:27:29.390 --> 00:27:31.730
there can be misrepresentations
in your dataset.

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:34.000
You might have more men than women,

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:36.423
so you don't learn much
about women and so on.

00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:40.687
And models of course can
further exacerbate bias

00:27:40.687 --> 00:27:43.810
in a very mathematical sense
that I'm going to omit.

00:27:43.810 --> 00:27:48.787
But all of these things can
very concretely lead to issues.

00:27:51.500 --> 00:27:52.600
But you can see that, you know,

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:53.890
these choices that we're making,

00:27:53.890 --> 00:27:56.283
we are making these choices as humans.

00:27:57.290 --> 00:27:59.300
Yes, in the end we design an algorithm,

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:01.093
which is a precise object,

00:28:02.984 --> 00:28:05.727
but these are the choices we're making.

00:28:05.727 --> 00:28:08.590
Let me give you another example of bias,

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:15.330
which is something other
than gender or race,

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:17.030
which we talk a lot about,

00:28:17.030 --> 00:28:19.680
something, which I mentioned earlier

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:22.500
in terms of polarization.

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:24.550
And this is bias in a different space,

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:27.270
it's in the space of opinions, okay.

00:28:27.270 --> 00:28:30.440
So imagine that this is you

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:35.440
and this is your Twitter or Facebook feed.

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:40.740
So, you know, let's say you log in now,

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:43.710
and between the last time
you logged in and now,

00:28:43.710 --> 00:28:48.600
many, many of the people who
you follow or your friends

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:51.100
would have generated posts, okay.

00:28:51.100 --> 00:28:54.180
So this is the set of all
posts that Facebook or Twitter

00:28:54.180 --> 00:28:56.700
can potentially show to you.

00:28:56.700 --> 00:28:57.940
And this is the key thing,

00:28:57.940 --> 00:29:00.030
this is their billion dollar algorithm,

00:29:00.030 --> 00:29:02.250
the content selection algorithm,

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:07.250
which from this viewed huge,
huge set for each individual

00:29:07.550 --> 00:29:09.423
creates a personalized feed.

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:16.580
And I have chosen to represent,
again, as a toy example,

00:29:16.650 --> 00:29:20.020
where the set of all documents
or set of all news articles,

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:23.750
or set of all tweets are
divided into two types;

00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:26.440
the red type and the blue type, okay.

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:29.020
The situation on the ground
is more complex, of course,

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:31.103
but let's say this is the case.

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:34.890
And this content selection
algorithm also gives you

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:36.980
a very limited ability

00:29:36.980 --> 00:29:39.150
to interact with your personalized feed.

00:29:39.150 --> 00:29:42.310
So you can say, I like
it, or I don't like it.

00:29:42.310 --> 00:29:45.360
I mean, for a very long
time this is what it was.

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:47.680
And now you can do
perhaps a little bit more.

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:52.043
So the reason why this
information was kept so simple

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:55.890
is because when the signal reaching

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.710
the content selection
algorithm was very clear.

00:29:58.710 --> 00:30:01.200
And so it could use this

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:05.650
next time when you come

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:09.120
to show you a personalized feed again.

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:10.910
So what is really happening?

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:12.084
So what is really happening

00:30:12.084 --> 00:30:16.000
in this content selection
algorithm is that

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:18.213
for each individual user,

00:30:19.886 --> 00:30:24.886
it represents each
individual user as a point

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:29.880
in some very high dimensional
space of opinions or articles.

00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:31.990
So there is this very
high dimensional space

00:30:31.990 --> 00:30:35.070
consisting of all possible articles

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:37.550
classified into red and blue.

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:41.310
And in the beginning, the
algorithm has no opinion of you,

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:44.690
no prior of you, so you start
off somewhere in the middle.

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:45.620
So when you start off,

00:30:45.620 --> 00:30:49.263
your feed will look kind of balanced.

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:52.640
But then you click on something.

00:30:52.640 --> 00:30:55.920
So when you click on something,
the algorithms thinks,

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:59.270
okay, this person seems to like this post

00:30:59.270 --> 00:31:01.190
and the post is red.

00:31:01.190 --> 00:31:05.280
So let me change it's representation

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:07.570
a little bit towards red.

00:31:07.570 --> 00:31:09.460
And this is a very precise movement

00:31:09.460 --> 00:31:11.283
that happens in this space.

00:31:12.230 --> 00:31:16.110
I mean, these dynamics
are governed by, you know,

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:17.430
the profit function.

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:19.130
So, you know, what is the goal ultimately?

00:31:19.130 --> 00:31:22.070
The goal is that the
content selection algorithm

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:23.090
should show you feeds,

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:27.570
which you will keep clicking on, right?

00:31:27.570 --> 00:31:29.433
And so that you don't go away.

00:31:31.040 --> 00:31:32.050
So they do that.

00:31:32.050 --> 00:31:34.460
So maybe now a new feed is generated,

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:36.360
and maybe now you click on a blue one.

00:31:38.150 --> 00:31:41.080
And so now you move a little
bit to the blue and so on.

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:42.482
So over a period of time,

00:31:42.482 --> 00:31:44.332
in a relatively short period of time,

00:31:47.380 --> 00:31:50.430
what we see happening is that two people

00:31:50.430 --> 00:31:53.200
starting from very close in the center

00:31:56.438 --> 00:31:58.080
their opinions can diverge.

00:31:58.080 --> 00:32:02.490
And you know, what that
really means is that,

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:03.910
from the algorithms point of view,

00:32:03.910 --> 00:32:06.743
you move into the deep
blue or deep red space.

00:32:07.690 --> 00:32:11.590
And after that, your feeds
are basically going to be

00:32:11.590 --> 00:32:13.510
just mostly blue or mostly red.

00:32:13.510 --> 00:32:15.500
I mean, take all this with
a pinch of salt, okay.

00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:20.500
So this is caricaturization
of what is really happening,

00:32:21.580 --> 00:32:23.730
but it's pretty close
to what is happening.

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:27.923
So what can we do?

00:32:29.421 --> 00:32:31.910
You know, these kinds
of biases are emerging.

00:32:31.910 --> 00:32:33.710
So what can we do?

00:32:33.710 --> 00:32:35.963
So we can focus on correcting data.

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:41.660
We can talk about
participatory design of models,

00:32:41.660 --> 00:32:44.270
you know, so we have to take into account

00:32:44.270 --> 00:32:46.980
not only Facebook or Twitter's preferences

00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:48.040
or Google's preferences,

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:50.370
but also preferences of the individuals,

00:32:50.370 --> 00:32:52.170
which has not been happening so far.

00:32:55.340 --> 00:33:00.340
Once we agree on the joint
goals or the joint models,

00:33:02.690 --> 00:33:05.090
we can try to design algorithms

00:33:05.090 --> 00:33:09.850
that are now constrained
to be fair in some sense.

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:14.850
And just like we have a
police and a legal system

00:33:15.280 --> 00:33:16.270
and a police system,

00:33:16.270 --> 00:33:20.283
which keeps auditing and doing
things to control people,

00:33:22.210 --> 00:33:26.680
we have these algorithmic audit units,

00:33:26.680 --> 00:33:29.990
or, you know, companies or whatever,

00:33:29.990 --> 00:33:34.630
mostly, you know, civic bodies,

00:33:34.630 --> 00:33:38.523
which are studying the impact
of algorithms in users.

00:33:44.990 --> 00:33:47.870
So there's a little problem though,

00:33:47.870 --> 00:33:51.730
that when I talk about fairness or bias,

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:55.165
you know, it's not just one thing, okay.

00:33:55.165 --> 00:33:59.435
It's many things possibly, right?

00:33:59.435 --> 00:34:02.854
So when I say something is not fair,

00:34:02.854 --> 00:34:04.950
it's not clear what it means.

00:34:04.950 --> 00:34:06.920
It may be clear to an individual

00:34:06.920 --> 00:34:08.890
who has experience in fairness,

00:34:08.890 --> 00:34:11.120
but when we talk about
designing algorithms,

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:13.070
we need to be precise, what do we mean?

00:34:15.102 --> 00:34:18.660
And what that really means is
writing down a precise metric,

00:34:18.660 --> 00:34:23.143
a precise way to measure
unfairness or bias.

00:34:24.130 --> 00:34:27.760
And this picture, which is still partial,

00:34:27.760 --> 00:34:30.450
kind of probably gives you a sense that

00:34:30.450 --> 00:34:31.357
things are very complex,

00:34:31.357 --> 00:34:34.400
the landscape of fairness is very complex.

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:37.440
There are many, many different
types of notions of fairness

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:38.657
that you can consider.

00:34:38.657 --> 00:34:41.210
But at least 40, which have legal backing

00:34:41.210 --> 00:34:43.080
in various contexts.

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:47.873
And in each context, there
are multiple stakeholders,

00:34:49.270 --> 00:34:51.260
and you can prove theorems,

00:34:51.260 --> 00:34:53.460
let's say that you
cannot design algorithms,

00:34:55.210 --> 00:34:58.910
which satisfy multiple
notions of fairness.

00:34:58.910 --> 00:35:01.310
So unfortunately you will
have to fix the context.

00:35:01.310 --> 00:35:03.580
And in that context, stick to a definition

00:35:03.580 --> 00:35:05.717
or notion of fairness, and then say,

00:35:05.717 --> 00:35:10.280
"Okay, let's design
algorithms in that framework,

00:35:10.280 --> 00:35:11.887
which are fair."

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:17.010
So this is where some of my work,

00:35:17.010 --> 00:35:20.223
which I've been doing over
the last few years comes in.

00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:27.590
And one of my main
contributions is to specify

00:35:28.540 --> 00:35:33.270
a very general framework for users

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:35.300
to encode their fairness constraints.

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:37.900
So we saw that the landscape
of fairness constraints

00:35:37.900 --> 00:35:39.800
can be very complex.

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:41.890
And what we did was to kind of figure out

00:35:41.890 --> 00:35:44.600
what's the simplest possible way

00:35:44.600 --> 00:35:48.080
for us to mathematically encode
these fairness constraints

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:52.080
and then design algorithms,

00:35:52.080 --> 00:35:54.510
which are constrained to be fair.

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:58.120
When you add constrains to a system,

00:35:58.120 --> 00:36:00.200
so this is an algorithm here,

00:36:00.200 --> 00:36:03.960
which is computing some
function from data.

00:36:03.960 --> 00:36:05.320
The moment you constrain it,

00:36:05.320 --> 00:36:07.670
a natural question arises is that

00:36:07.670 --> 00:36:10.130
the previous algorithm
is not going to work.

00:36:10.130 --> 00:36:14.600
So who will design this new algorithm

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:17.010
and whether it's even possible

00:36:17.010 --> 00:36:19.490
to design an efficient algorithm.

00:36:19.490 --> 00:36:21.410
You know, so efficiency
is the keyword here,

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:25.840
because you can write down a function,

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:27.800
which you would like to compute,

00:36:27.800 --> 00:36:31.049
but then if it's not
efficiently computable,

00:36:31.049 --> 00:36:33.500
it's not going to happen
in the sense that,

00:36:33.500 --> 00:36:35.670
you know, the size of data and the scale

00:36:35.670 --> 00:36:40.630
at which we are operating
is so huge that the problem

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:42.930
should also come with
some efficient algorithms.

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:44.230
Otherwise, you know,

00:36:44.230 --> 00:36:47.803
you will have to pay some
computational price of fairness.

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:52.600
On the other hand, from the
point of view of let's say

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:54.140
a company like Twitter,

00:36:54.140 --> 00:36:55.790
if you say constrain
Twitter's feed, right?

00:36:55.790 --> 00:36:58.407
So you give some controls
to the user and say,

00:36:58.407 --> 00:37:01.110
"The user will control to
the extent of the content

00:37:01.110 --> 00:37:02.630
that they want to see,"

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:05.637
then Twitter will object
immediately and say,

00:37:05.637 --> 00:37:07.660
"You know, but now we don't know

00:37:07.660 --> 00:37:09.500
what the engagement metrics will be,

00:37:09.500 --> 00:37:11.960
and that can really wipe
us out of this market.

00:37:11.960 --> 00:37:14.000
So if we're not going to agree to it."

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:17.260
So this is what you make
think of as price of fairness.

00:37:17.260 --> 00:37:19.090
Fairness is a great goal.

00:37:19.090 --> 00:37:20.620
But in this capitalistic society,

00:37:20.620 --> 00:37:22.330
who pays the price of fairness,

00:37:22.330 --> 00:37:24.470
who will pay the price of fairness?

00:37:24.470 --> 00:37:28.260
So these are very non-trivial
and important questions.

00:37:28.260 --> 00:37:33.260
And my work has address the whole pipeline

00:37:33.610 --> 00:37:38.380
starting from identifying
bias to fixing data,

00:37:38.380 --> 00:37:39.680
figuring out the right models,

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:43.263
developing fair algorithms,
and studying impact.

00:37:44.940 --> 00:37:49.470
And so not all algorithms,

00:37:49.470 --> 00:37:51.770
oops, okay, so maybe I skipped a slide.

00:37:51.770 --> 00:37:54.390
Not out all algorithms are equal.

00:37:54.390 --> 00:37:56.683
Some algorithms are very high stakes.

00:37:59.421 --> 00:38:00.254
I mean, you know, for example,

00:38:00.254 --> 00:38:04.620
the algorithm used to schedule
the fleet of Hertz Car Rental

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:08.300
perhaps has less impact,
you know, at a scale

00:38:08.300 --> 00:38:12.630
than something like what you
see in Twitter or Facebook,

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:14.484
or something that you
see in Google search,

00:38:14.484 --> 00:38:19.484
or classification
algorithms, or, you know,

00:38:21.317 --> 00:38:22.240
or other stuff.

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:25.190
So I've worked on some of
the high stakes applications

00:38:25.190 --> 00:38:28.460
and come up with interesting solutions.

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:30.180
You can see more about them.

00:38:30.180 --> 00:38:32.940
Many of the solutions
actually come with new demos.

00:38:32.940 --> 00:38:35.840
Once we design the
algorithm, we code it up,

00:38:35.840 --> 00:38:38.010
we create a demo for you to see,

00:38:38.010 --> 00:38:39.470
and if you're really curious,

00:38:39.470 --> 00:38:42.020
you can go to this website
and play around with it.

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:46.580
So just to wrap back and say, okay,

00:38:46.580 --> 00:38:48.930
so what does the picture look like

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:51.800
in this world of personalization?

00:38:51.800 --> 00:38:55.130
Conceptually, it looks like the following:

00:38:55.130 --> 00:38:57.750
that our framework
constrains the algorithm

00:38:57.750 --> 00:39:02.000
to not let people go
beyond a certain point.

00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:04.313
I mean, in retrospect
it seems very simple,

00:39:05.510 --> 00:39:09.560
but the hard part is in
designing new algorithms,

00:39:09.560 --> 00:39:11.740
which respect these constraints.

00:39:11.740 --> 00:39:16.740
So that's the main
contribution of this work.

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:22.200
So all of this was
assuming that we know data,

00:39:23.010 --> 00:39:24.763
we know that the data is accurate.

00:39:28.390 --> 00:39:31.040
Once you start looking
deeper into the system,

00:39:31.040 --> 00:39:33.780
one of the key questions or key issues

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:37.893
that we see is that the
data may not be accurate.

00:39:38.770 --> 00:39:42.260
So besides historical and societal biases,

00:39:42.260 --> 00:39:44.230
there may be other factors,

00:39:44.230 --> 00:39:49.230
which actually come into the way of data.

00:39:51.770 --> 00:39:53.710
So let me now explain that.

00:39:53.710 --> 00:39:57.410
And now we are going
towards understanding bias

00:39:57.410 --> 00:40:00.190
in human decision-making from
algorithmic decision-making.

00:40:00.190 --> 00:40:02.067
So we'll make a transition

00:40:02.067 --> 00:40:05.470
and then I think in the
next 10 to 15 minutes,

00:40:05.470 --> 00:40:09.513
I should be able to
wrap up, 10, 12 minutes.

00:40:14.050 --> 00:40:15.850
So Priya, are we taking
the questions at the end

00:40:15.850 --> 00:40:17.823
or in the middle?

00:40:20.790 --> 00:40:22.087
- If you are open to--

00:40:23.704 --> 00:40:26.820
- I think this is a good
point to pause for a minute

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:31.820
and see if anybody has questions
about the previous part.

00:40:31.820 --> 00:40:33.280
I'm open, yes.

00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:37.410
- Okay, I haven't seen anything
up here in the Q&amp;A yet,

00:40:37.410 --> 00:40:39.770
but we can encourage them.

00:40:39.770 --> 00:40:43.210
The only note that I
