00:40:43.210 --> 00:40:45.650
Christina's pointed out, I
mean there are lots of people

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:46.740
who are interested,

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:49.393
actually would like to
have the links posted.

00:40:50.500 --> 00:40:51.573
- Okay.
- Yeah.

00:40:52.630 --> 00:40:54.810
- Okay, so I'll keep on going

00:40:54.810 --> 00:40:56.920
and, you know, think about questions

00:40:56.920 --> 00:40:59.450
and feel free to
interrupt or ask any time.

00:40:59.450 --> 00:41:02.260
I mean, I think it's important to hear--

00:41:02.260 --> 00:41:07.260
- I actually, if I may, I
wanted to ask a quick question.

00:41:07.300 --> 00:41:11.790
So, you know, it's really
sort of remarkably clear

00:41:11.790 --> 00:41:15.510
exposition, Nisheeth of how
our sort of inference systems

00:41:15.510 --> 00:41:17.700
are biased and how in turn these results

00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:20.740
that are recovered from
these and the claims whatever

00:41:20.740 --> 00:41:22.070
can change us, right.

00:41:22.070 --> 00:41:24.410
So there's the sort of the
power of the algorithms

00:41:24.410 --> 00:41:27.310
that while they encode our bias,

00:41:27.310 --> 00:41:29.940
they may also hold the key
to mitigating bias, right?

00:41:29.940 --> 00:41:32.840
So they're sort of, you know,

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:36.090
epistemically, they serve
as sort of exposers, right?

00:41:36.090 --> 00:41:39.330
They're very effective
exposers of human bias.

00:41:39.330 --> 00:41:41.890
So one thing I was curious was, you know,

00:41:41.890 --> 00:41:44.760
when you talked about
assigning scores, for example,

00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:46.260
we were looking at it, right?

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:50.270
So how much of the bias
really comes from the fact

00:41:50.270 --> 00:41:52.710
that, you know, mathematical
scores, et cetera,

00:41:52.710 --> 00:41:54.910
have an implicit ordering, right?

00:41:54.910 --> 00:41:57.300
Like five is better than 10 or whatever.

00:41:57.300 --> 00:42:01.230
So what if we replace at the top layer,

00:42:01.230 --> 00:42:03.640
I don't know, I had sort
of a lame suggestion,

00:42:03.640 --> 00:42:04.820
why not just have colors or, you know,

00:42:04.820 --> 00:42:07.260
something that you can, you know,

00:42:07.260 --> 00:42:09.463
at a deeper layer map into numbers?

00:42:10.526 --> 00:42:11.380
- That's a great question.

00:42:11.380 --> 00:42:14.400
So, yes, I mean, I think
that's a very good question

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:17.810
and the answer to this
is not entirely obvious

00:42:17.810 --> 00:42:21.130
because as I already
hinted that, you know,

00:42:21.130 --> 00:42:23.320
you can design some beautiful systems,

00:42:23.320 --> 00:42:26.540
which are supposed to do
what they're supposed to do,

00:42:26.540 --> 00:42:29.750
but in the end you will
also need an algorithm

00:42:29.750 --> 00:42:32.200
to find the parameters of the model.

00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:33.100
- [Priya] Yeah.

00:42:33.100 --> 00:42:36.200
- So much of this stuff is constrained

00:42:36.200 --> 00:42:40.130
by that aspect of things.

00:42:40.130 --> 00:42:43.180
So the reason to go into
this continuous space

00:42:43.180 --> 00:42:46.110
of order numbers, you
know, simplify everything,

00:42:46.110 --> 00:42:49.250
the real numbers, not even discreet.

00:42:49.250 --> 00:42:51.390
I mean, in the world of discreet numbers,

00:42:51.390 --> 00:42:53.370
there's still more you can do

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:55.770
than in the world of continuous numbers.

00:42:55.770 --> 00:43:00.220
So the thing is that these algorithms,

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:03.750
which optimized are
essentially algorithms,

00:43:03.750 --> 00:43:06.350
which work in continuous spaces,

00:43:06.350 --> 00:43:08.930
things like gradient
descent, gradient flows.

00:43:08.930 --> 00:43:12.868
I mean, these are inspired again
from physics in some sense.

00:43:12.868 --> 00:43:17.868
And yeah, I mean, this is
the set we are working with.

00:43:17.870 --> 00:43:20.790
So, you know, if you face a problem today

00:43:20.790 --> 00:43:24.800
and let's say you are an engineer
who's working in a company

00:43:24.800 --> 00:43:27.970
and whose manager gives the
task to design something,

00:43:27.970 --> 00:43:31.250
you're not in a position
to reinvent an algorithm.

00:43:31.250 --> 00:43:32.330
- [Priya] Right.

00:43:32.330 --> 00:43:34.980
- So you will just use
off-the-shelf algorithm

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:38.250
and you know, you'll just
do the minimum thing.

00:43:38.250 --> 00:43:40.620
You say, "Okay, let's
just encode everything

00:43:40.620 --> 00:43:44.220
as a real number and ordered in some sense

00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:45.600
without putting so much thought

00:43:45.600 --> 00:43:47.740
into what impact your decisions have

00:43:47.740 --> 00:43:49.720
on the lives of others."

00:43:49.720 --> 00:43:54.590
And this also has to do
with a complete failure

00:43:54.590 --> 00:43:56.660
of the education system
in some sense, right?

00:43:56.660 --> 00:43:59.930
I mean, I must say that
I take responsibility

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:04.640
because, you know, we teach
people design of algorithms,

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:06.420
data structures, and so on,

00:44:06.420 --> 00:44:08.560
but you know, a decade ago

00:44:08.560 --> 00:44:11.360
nobody in their lecture would even mention

00:44:11.360 --> 00:44:16.360
the potential possibility of the impact

00:44:16.510 --> 00:44:20.530
that the choices will have.

00:44:20.530 --> 00:44:24.850
And now I teach a course
in which I make people

00:44:24.850 --> 00:44:26.970
at least be more aware of the assumptions

00:44:26.970 --> 00:44:28.970
and their potential impacts.

00:44:28.970 --> 00:44:30.240
We still do the same thing,

00:44:30.240 --> 00:44:32.920
but we just make more aware of the impact.

00:44:38.350 --> 00:44:42.580
- Just changing around the
categories of representation

00:44:42.580 --> 00:44:46.740
is not enough A, because
there are too many algorithms

00:44:46.740 --> 00:44:49.190
lying around, people will just use them.

00:44:49.190 --> 00:44:53.080
You have to completely start
with a new set of categories,

00:44:53.080 --> 00:44:55.270
but I guess the ultimate
limit, as you said, you know,

00:44:55.270 --> 00:44:58.570
is that we have a well-defined
mathematical space

00:44:58.570 --> 00:44:59.403
in which we live.

00:44:59.403 --> 00:45:00.633
- Right.

00:45:00.633 --> 00:45:02.670
I mean, but it's also a
very challenging question.

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:04.920
I mean, you know, how
do you capture a human?

00:45:07.835 --> 00:45:09.490
I mean, all said and done,

00:45:09.490 --> 00:45:11.340
I mean, if you want to implement this

00:45:11.340 --> 00:45:14.220
on some kind of computer, then
going back to Turing's work.

00:45:14.220 --> 00:45:18.270
I mean, Turing proved that any computer

00:45:18.270 --> 00:45:20.350
is equivalent to the doing machine, right?

00:45:20.350 --> 00:45:23.800
So at some level you will have to encode

00:45:25.330 --> 00:45:30.330
something as complex as human
in let's say a binary form

00:45:31.540 --> 00:45:34.983
or I mean, some representation, right?

00:45:41.854 --> 00:45:44.960
So there is a lot to say
here and a lot to do here.

00:45:44.960 --> 00:45:46.610
So by no means I'm pretending

00:45:46.610 --> 00:45:47.900
that we have solved any problem.

00:45:47.900 --> 00:45:49.690
In fact, in this part of my talk,

00:45:49.690 --> 00:45:52.490
I'm gonna tell you how we
did not solve that problem

00:45:52.490 --> 00:45:54.710
that we aim to solve actually.

00:45:54.710 --> 00:45:56.840
And the reason, if I may continue, Priya,

00:45:56.840 --> 00:45:58.469
- Yeah, please go ahead, yeah, yeah.

00:45:58.469 --> 00:46:03.469
- Is that so far, I assume that data.

00:46:03.690 --> 00:46:06.810
So all of these algorithms
that we designed work

00:46:06.810 --> 00:46:10.840
as long as the data is accurate.

00:46:10.840 --> 00:46:12.980
So forget the issue that, you know,

00:46:12.980 --> 00:46:15.763
women might have historically
lower salary than men,

00:46:17.950 --> 00:46:21.930
but we do assume that the salary

00:46:21.930 --> 00:46:23.360
is what it is being reported.

00:46:23.360 --> 00:46:24.900
I mean, you know, that
the person is reporting

00:46:24.900 --> 00:46:26.070
the right salary, right?

00:46:26.070 --> 00:46:29.403
Or we are measuring information correctly,

00:46:30.520 --> 00:46:35.520
but as we delve more into
human-generated data,

00:46:35.820 --> 00:46:37.950
it becomes even more clear

00:46:37.950 --> 00:46:42.393
that the data that we
record is first of all,

00:46:44.310 --> 00:46:49.310
not necessarily close to the
data that we should report,

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:54.150
even if you want to record
somebody that ethnicity, right,

00:46:54.150 --> 00:46:55.693
it's not clear that when we look at it,

00:46:55.693 --> 00:46:59.060
when we record and we do
have that person set history.

00:46:59.980 --> 00:47:02.130
I mean, it's like as a physicist,

00:47:02.130 --> 00:47:03.780
you can understand this probably.

00:47:04.970 --> 00:47:06.790
I mean, you wish you had the true data,

00:47:06.790 --> 00:47:09.620
but you know, data traveling
over billions of years

00:47:09.620 --> 00:47:11.700
coming from very far,
and then add some noise,

00:47:11.700 --> 00:47:14.210
and, you know, all kinds of
things might happen to that.

00:47:14.210 --> 00:47:16.220
So in this context,

00:47:16.220 --> 00:47:20.573
what we have been seeing is
due to a variety of reasons.

00:47:21.690 --> 00:47:25.700
For instance, you know, often
we need ethnic information,

00:47:25.700 --> 00:47:27.440
but we don't have ethnic information

00:47:27.440 --> 00:47:31.150
because it may be illegal
to have ethnic information

00:47:31.150 --> 00:47:32.350
to record.

00:47:32.350 --> 00:47:33.183
So what do we do?

00:47:33.183 --> 00:47:36.790
We impute ethnic information
from other things.

00:47:36.790 --> 00:47:39.100
So that gives rise to a noisy estimate

00:47:39.100 --> 00:47:42.670
of somebody's ethnic information, okay.

00:47:42.670 --> 00:47:45.053
Maybe because of privacy concerns,

00:47:46.474 --> 00:47:48.883
you might have to add noise to data.

00:47:50.582 --> 00:47:53.700
And what I wanted to focus
on in this rest of the talk

00:47:55.830 --> 00:47:59.530
in this little 10 minutes
is implicit biases.

00:47:59.530 --> 00:48:03.170
So much of this data also comes from

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:06.790
applying certain evaluation
processes on individuals.

00:48:06.790 --> 00:48:09.850
So for instance, SAT scores.

00:48:09.850 --> 00:48:12.250
I mean, we here at Yale
are in the business

00:48:12.250 --> 00:48:15.150
of taking decisions
about students, faculty,

00:48:15.150 --> 00:48:16.573
everybody all the time.

00:48:18.384 --> 00:48:20.750
You know, we want to recruit individuals

00:48:20.750 --> 00:48:23.603
for some vaguely specified goals,

00:48:25.290 --> 00:48:28.630
but we choose metrics
that's SAT score-based

00:48:28.630 --> 00:48:29.900
that and the other,

00:48:29.900 --> 00:48:32.100
there is a whole evaluation process.

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:35.903
And these evaluation processes
can have implicit biases.

00:48:36.940 --> 00:48:40.000
And the punchline is
that much of the stuff

00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:42.930
that I talked about this first generation

00:48:42.930 --> 00:48:44.910
study of algorithmic bias

00:48:46.360 --> 00:48:49.920
actually does not seem to work well

00:48:49.920 --> 00:48:51.310
when data is perturbed.

00:48:51.310 --> 00:48:52.860
And Alexander Madre,

00:48:52.860 --> 00:48:57.180
who is an expert in designing algorithms

00:48:57.180 --> 00:49:00.850
for perturb data hopefully
we'll tell you more

00:49:00.850 --> 00:49:03.963
about this on Monday.

00:49:05.590 --> 00:49:08.450
So let me just talk about
one type of perturbation,

00:49:08.450 --> 00:49:10.453
which is implicit biases.

00:49:11.600 --> 00:49:12.930
And this was the punchline.

00:49:12.930 --> 00:49:15.760
you know, even if your intention

00:49:15.760 --> 00:49:17.850
was to correct for fairness,

00:49:17.850 --> 00:49:20.530
if it failed to take into
account the precise way

00:49:20.530 --> 00:49:24.597
in which that data can be perturbed,

00:49:26.197 --> 00:49:28.577
you know, the cure can
be worse than the cause.

00:49:29.570 --> 00:49:30.660
Is that the right saying?

00:49:30.660 --> 00:49:31.693
I think so.

00:49:34.090 --> 00:49:36.810
No, the effect could
be the worst than cure

00:49:36.810 --> 00:49:38.630
or something like that, yeah.

00:49:38.630 --> 00:49:40.180
Sorry, I'm getting a bit tired.

00:49:41.210 --> 00:49:46.210
So implicit bias is
rampant in the real world.

00:49:46.805 --> 00:49:48.190
There is lots of evidence.

00:49:48.190 --> 00:49:51.660
I'm not qualified to do these experiments,

00:49:51.660 --> 00:49:56.660
but there are very prominent
economists and sociologists

00:49:57.870 --> 00:49:59.650
who do this.

00:49:59.650 --> 00:50:04.480
And in almost all aspects
of our social interactions,

00:50:04.480 --> 00:50:06.940
we see rampant implicit bias

00:50:06.940 --> 00:50:11.610
to just give an example,
which is relevant to us.

00:50:11.610 --> 00:50:12.980
I think there were studies done

00:50:12.980 --> 00:50:16.343
where emails were sent
to Ivy League professors.

00:50:17.460 --> 00:50:20.900
The content of the email
was exactly the same

00:50:20.900 --> 00:50:22.560
except in some emails,

00:50:22.560 --> 00:50:25.920
the names name was changed
from a male to a female,

00:50:25.920 --> 00:50:28.270
to a white sounding name,
a black sounding name.

00:50:28.270 --> 00:50:30.500
And there was significant differences

00:50:30.500 --> 00:50:33.150
in the percentages of responses.

00:50:33.150 --> 00:50:35.410
Now, one can always argue

00:50:35.410 --> 00:50:38.110
and try to explain each
individual data point.

00:50:38.110 --> 00:50:40.510
But when there are so many people

00:50:40.510 --> 00:50:44.410
who study this and find systematic issues,

00:50:44.410 --> 00:50:47.800
one cannot deny that there is
something called implicit bias

00:50:47.800 --> 00:50:50.930
in this world, which is really
the unconscious attribution

00:50:50.930 --> 00:50:52.530
of qualities or lack there off

00:50:52.530 --> 00:50:54.143
of members of particular groups.

00:50:55.430 --> 00:50:59.640
So the bottom line is
that what you observe,

00:50:59.640 --> 00:51:02.630
the data that you observe
may not be accurate.

00:51:02.630 --> 00:51:04.580
So let's look at a very simple problem,

00:51:05.880 --> 00:51:10.490
which now starts moving from a problem,

00:51:10.490 --> 00:51:15.020
which you may not associate
necessarily to computer science,

00:51:15.020 --> 00:51:17.510
but nevertheless is still
an algorithmic problem.

00:51:17.510 --> 00:51:20.440
And many of us here

00:51:20.440 --> 00:51:24.470
might have actually tried
to solve this problem.

00:51:24.470 --> 00:51:25.830
So the problem is very simple.

00:51:25.830 --> 00:51:27.240
Again, it's a toy problem.

00:51:27.240 --> 00:51:29.840
Don't take any of this
stuff too seriously,

00:51:29.840 --> 00:51:32.240
the goal is to explain the point.

00:51:32.240 --> 00:51:33.497
Suppose you have M individuals,

00:51:33.497 --> 00:51:35.410
let's say hundred individuals.

00:51:35.410 --> 00:51:37.300
In this case, one, two, three, four,

00:51:37.300 --> 00:51:39.713
five, six, seven, eight individuals,

00:51:40.860 --> 00:51:44.030
and each individual has
some latent utility.

00:51:44.030 --> 00:51:46.620
So this is the true
utility of an individual,

00:51:46.620 --> 00:51:47.593
whatever it is.

00:51:49.290 --> 00:51:53.610
And I have four interview spots, okay.

00:51:53.610 --> 00:51:57.460
So I have maybe one faculty
spot in my department,

00:51:57.460 --> 00:52:00.623
but I want to short list
four people to interview.

00:52:01.870 --> 00:52:03.003
So what shall I do?

00:52:04.895 --> 00:52:05.800
You know, as Priya said,

00:52:05.800 --> 00:52:08.010
one of the reasons why we're
including them as numbers

00:52:08.010 --> 00:52:09.293
is that we can compare.

00:52:10.220 --> 00:52:15.090
So the higher the number the better it is.

00:52:15.090 --> 00:52:20.090
So our goal is to shortlist
these four people or n people,

00:52:20.260 --> 00:52:23.800
which maximize the
cumulative latent utility.

00:52:23.800 --> 00:52:25.910
So this has a very simple
algorithm actually,

00:52:25.910 --> 00:52:27.423
which I think everybody here,

00:52:28.440 --> 00:52:30.090
you don't need to take an algorithms class

00:52:30.090 --> 00:52:31.890
to design such an algorithm,

00:52:31.890 --> 00:52:34.500
which is to first find
the one with the maximum,

00:52:34.500 --> 00:52:36.768
remove them from the
set, find the next one,

00:52:36.768 --> 00:52:39.080
remove them from the
set, find the third one,

00:52:39.080 --> 00:52:41.530
remove them from the
set, find the fourth one.

00:52:41.530 --> 00:52:43.040
That's your set of four people

00:52:43.040 --> 00:52:46.520
that you would like to interview, okay.

00:52:46.520 --> 00:52:47.360
This is very simple,

00:52:47.360 --> 00:52:49.393
even a child can solve this problem.

00:52:51.764 --> 00:52:56.764
The issue with this is that it assumes

00:52:58.000 --> 00:53:00.080
that these blue numbers here that you see,

00:53:00.080 --> 00:53:02.330
which are the true
utilities are the real ones

00:53:02.330 --> 00:53:05.838
that we're seeing, okay.

00:53:05.838 --> 00:53:10.838
But in reality, if we are
suffering from implicit bias,

00:53:13.004 --> 00:53:17.813
we might not see this two
utility or latent utility.

00:53:18.890 --> 00:53:23.253
So here's a very simple
mathematical model of implicit bias,

00:53:24.400 --> 00:53:27.830
which is suppose there
is some parameter, beta,

00:53:27.830 --> 00:53:31.373
you can think of beta, let's
say 0.9 or in this case half.

00:53:32.850 --> 00:53:35.460
And for every person in a certain group,

00:53:35.460 --> 00:53:37.890
you view their utility to you.

00:53:37.890 --> 00:53:40.460
The observed utility to yourself

00:53:40.460 --> 00:53:42.760
is half of the true utility.

00:53:42.760 --> 00:53:43.820
Of course, you don't know that.

00:53:43.820 --> 00:53:47.210
You're not doing this
process, you know, explicitly.

00:53:47.210 --> 00:53:48.280
So this is my view.

00:53:48.280 --> 00:53:49.630
So I'm let's say the person

00:53:49.630 --> 00:53:52.883
who can observe the entire thing.

00:53:53.870 --> 00:53:56.000
So I mean, you know, as an individual

00:53:56.000 --> 00:53:58.700
who's making this call,
you're not aware of this beta.

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:01.580
If you were, then you would
correct it, but you're not.

00:54:01.580 --> 00:54:04.710
So you just view this person
as being useful to you

00:54:04.710 --> 00:54:08.940
to the extent of five units
as opposed to 10 units.

00:54:08.940 --> 00:54:10.240
So now what would you do?

00:54:10.240 --> 00:54:11.713
I mean, what would you do?

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:14.820
You would use the same algorithm again,

00:54:14.820 --> 00:54:17.270
but with the perturbed dataset, right?

00:54:17.270 --> 00:54:19.990
So instead of the true utility
of this person being 18,

00:54:19.990 --> 00:54:21.350
now it's nine.

00:54:21.350 --> 00:54:23.963
So now when you go and
select the top four,

00:54:25.210 --> 00:54:27.180
instead of selecting the top four,

00:54:27.180 --> 00:54:29.660
which maximize your latent utility,

00:54:29.660 --> 00:54:31.630
you maximize the observed utility.

00:54:31.630 --> 00:54:34.460
And these are not the
same numbers anymore.

00:54:34.460 --> 00:54:36.560
So this is a strange situation

00:54:36.560 --> 00:54:39.720
because it's actually good for everybody

00:54:39.720 --> 00:54:41.013
if you can see the truth,

00:54:41.910 --> 00:54:43.440
because you just don't know

00:54:43.440 --> 00:54:45.940
that this person actually
has higher utility for you,

00:54:45.940 --> 00:54:47.110
because of your biases.

00:54:47.110 --> 00:54:49.400
Your biases are stopping
you from doing this.

00:54:49.400 --> 00:54:50.573
So what can we do?

00:54:55.960 --> 00:54:57.670
One can do anti-bias training.

00:54:57.670 --> 00:55:01.570
I mean, this is the traditional
solution that you can apply.

00:55:01.570 --> 00:55:03.463
It has limited efficacy.

00:55:07.320 --> 00:55:09.840
There's actually a very
interesting solution

00:55:09.840 --> 00:55:12.000
to aversion of this problem,

00:55:12.000 --> 00:55:13.080
which is called the Rooney rule,

00:55:13.080 --> 00:55:14.660
which has been used

00:55:14.660 --> 00:55:17.820
in the context of the
National Football League here.

00:55:17.820 --> 00:55:20.710
So the Rooney rule roughly says that

00:55:20.710 --> 00:55:24.160
in the process of
selecting who to interview

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:25.790
for head coach of an NFL team,

00:55:25.790 --> 00:55:29.210
you must interview at
least one black person,

00:55:29.210 --> 00:55:30.453
if I get it correctly.

00:55:31.710 --> 00:55:36.350
And there is evidence,
at least in the media,

00:55:36.350 --> 00:55:40.060
that this seems to be
a useful thing to do.

00:55:40.060 --> 00:55:42.610
So the question is, is it
really in this simple problem,

00:55:42.610 --> 00:55:45.683
does it really work, and
if so, to what extent?

00:55:46.780 --> 00:55:51.440
So what we did recently is
to look at the following

00:55:51.440 --> 00:55:54.350
intervention algorithm.

00:55:54.350 --> 00:55:58.060
So now we say, okay, so
there's a new parameter

00:55:58.060 --> 00:56:02.430
that we introduced L, and we
say, select at least L people

00:56:02.430 --> 00:56:04.660
from this disadvantaged group.

00:56:04.660 --> 00:56:07.110
So even though you don't
know your implicit biases,

00:56:07.110 --> 00:56:10.340
I can still constrain
you, as a policymaker,

00:56:10.340 --> 00:56:13.640
to say that you must select
at least let's say two people

00:56:13.640 --> 00:56:16.403
from this set of women to interview.

00:56:17.720 --> 00:56:20.173
And what we can show is that,

00:56:21.430 --> 00:56:25.230
you know, you can pick
a value of L carefully.

00:56:25.230 --> 00:56:27.570
Then once I've fixed
this as a policymaker,

00:56:27.570 --> 00:56:32.210
then you're still doing
whatever you're doing

00:56:32.210 --> 00:56:37.210
with an implicit biases
can recover the right set.

00:56:39.846 --> 00:56:41.720
So this might sound a
bit counter-intuitive,

00:56:41.720 --> 00:56:45.863
but there is a nice mathematical
explanation for this.

00:56:47.140 --> 00:56:51.590
So we can come up with interesting
algorithmic interventions

00:56:51.590 --> 00:56:53.073
in these situations.

00:56:54.090 --> 00:56:55.507
One can also ask, you know,

00:56:55.507 --> 00:56:58.320
"Okay, so it seems to
solve the problem locally,

00:56:58.320 --> 00:57:00.480
what about the long-term impact?"

00:57:00.480 --> 00:57:03.880
So we can model this process
of the dynamical system

00:57:04.980 --> 00:57:07.593
and also do experiments with people.

00:57:08.850 --> 00:57:13.850
And we can show that, you know,

00:57:16.210 --> 00:57:18.810
under a simple and well established rule

00:57:18.810 --> 00:57:21.110
of how humans change their opinions,

00:57:21.110 --> 00:57:24.150
you know, which is modeled
to Bayesian learning,

00:57:24.150 --> 00:57:26.350
the Rooney rule over time,

00:57:26.350 --> 00:57:29.790
significantly impacts the implicit bias.

00:57:29.790 --> 00:57:34.687
So we come up with a new
way, at least, you know.

00:57:36.500 --> 00:57:40.120
We come up with a new way as a suggestion

00:57:40.120 --> 00:57:44.320
to do this kind of training
against implicit bias.

00:57:44.320 --> 00:57:45.420
Anyway, so if you're interested,

00:57:45.420 --> 00:57:48.090
you can look at some of
our work on this stuff.

00:57:48.090 --> 00:57:51.183
I'm just going to end now,
because I think I'm out of time.

00:57:53.729 --> 00:57:55.893
So what did we see in this talk?

00:57:57.130 --> 00:57:59.010
So one of the first things is that,

00:57:59.010 --> 00:58:04.010
you know, human decisions
can appear in this objective

00:58:05.530 --> 00:58:08.820
or seemingly objective world of algorithms

00:58:10.150 --> 00:58:11.713
and make them biased.

00:58:12.600 --> 00:58:14.490
Bias can mean very different things

00:58:14.490 --> 00:58:15.950
in very different contexts.

00:58:15.950 --> 00:58:18.960
And sometimes it may be impossible

00:58:18.960 --> 00:58:21.600
to satisfy all the constraints of bias

00:58:21.600 --> 00:58:25.253
in algorithmic situations and
also real world situations.

00:58:26.400 --> 00:58:30.010
And there's a great demand
to design algorithms,

00:58:30.010 --> 00:58:33.370
given the rampantness of these algorithms

00:58:33.370 --> 00:58:36.280
and the impact they
have already on society.

00:58:36.280 --> 00:58:39.560
There's a great movement going on,

00:58:39.560 --> 00:58:41.750
at least in certain communities,

00:58:41.750 --> 00:58:45.860
which is thinking about redesigning

00:58:45.860 --> 00:58:48.470
some of these high stakes algorithms

00:58:48.470 --> 00:58:52.023
to keep in mind the context
and the stakeholders.

00:58:53.300 --> 00:58:58.300
And finally, an understanding
of algorithmic bias

00:58:58.500 --> 00:59:01.600
cannot only inform the design of fair

00:59:01.600 --> 00:59:04.030
or unbias artificial algorithms,

00:59:04.030 --> 00:59:08.010
but if you view policy
decisions as also algorithms

00:59:08.950 --> 00:59:11.070
acting on populations,

00:59:11.070 --> 00:59:13.260
then it also gives us insights

00:59:13.260 --> 00:59:16.420
into how to redesign policies.

00:59:16.420 --> 00:59:21.420
And some of our work is
heading in that direction.

00:59:24.750 --> 00:59:28.010
You can see more of my
work on this website,

00:59:28.010 --> 00:59:29.470
Controlling Bias.

00:59:29.470 --> 00:59:32.290
And Priya already mentioned, you know,

00:59:32.290 --> 00:59:34.890
this is not an MBA where
we reach only technical

00:59:34.890 --> 00:59:39.670
or mathematical problems can
lead you to good solutions.

00:59:39.670 --> 00:59:43.833
So here at Yale, we have a
collective, I like to think of,

00:59:45.010 --> 00:59:47.360
we call this Computation
and Society Initiative,

00:59:47.360 --> 00:59:50.480
a bunch of like-minded
people who come together

00:59:50.480 --> 00:59:53.350
to try to understand how we
can address these problems

00:59:53.350 --> 00:59:55.930
from a multidisciplinary point of view.

00:59:55.930 --> 01:00:00.230
And I would stop here and
thank you for your attention,

01:00:00.230 --> 01:00:02.440
and Priya, it all over to you.

01:00:02.440 --> 01:00:04.440
- Yep, thank you so much, Nisheeth.

01:00:04.440 --> 01:00:06.670
This was absolutely wonderful.

01:00:06.670 --> 01:00:09.310
What a clear exposition.

01:00:09.310 --> 01:00:13.130
I think as always, you know,
every conversation with you,

01:00:13.130 --> 01:00:15.590
I learn new things.

01:00:15.590 --> 01:00:18.730
And I think what you have
clearly showed us is, you know,

01:00:18.730 --> 01:00:23.730
epistemically, algorithms serve
as very effective exposers

01:00:25.280 --> 01:00:30.100
of bias, and that we can
also in turn use them

01:00:30.100 --> 01:00:33.270
as devices to mitigate the bias in turn.

01:00:33.270 --> 01:00:35.800
So they are sort of very, very powerful

01:00:35.800 --> 01:00:38.420
and useful at the same time.

01:00:38.420 --> 01:00:40.740
So I think we have one question in the Q&amp;A

01:00:40.740 --> 01:00:41.860
that I will read out,

01:00:41.860 --> 01:00:45.180
and then I of course
have a couple of my own,

01:00:45.180 --> 01:00:48.040
but I will hold my
horses for a little bit.

01:00:48.040 --> 01:00:51.500
So our first question is from
one of our regular attendees,

01:00:51.500 --> 01:00:52.333
Jenny.

01:00:52.333 --> 01:00:53.250
Hi, Jenny.

01:00:53.250 --> 01:00:57.817
And her question is instead of
constraining the maximum bias

01:00:59.140 --> 01:01:03.910
a user can arrive at, could
you also imagine other ways

01:01:03.910 --> 01:01:07.130
