01:01:07.130 --> 01:01:10.300
For example, showing other random contents

01:01:10.300 --> 01:01:13.323
or even the opposite of
what they liked before.

01:01:14.290 --> 01:01:16.590
This could also stimulate new interests.

01:01:16.590 --> 01:01:18.600
well, I guess she's referring possibly

01:01:18.600 --> 01:01:20.912
to the polarization example.

01:01:20.912 --> 01:01:21.745
- Right, right.

01:01:21.745 --> 01:01:23.832
So I think I understand the question.

01:01:23.832 --> 01:01:24.957
Thank you, Priya for reading it

01:01:24.957 --> 01:01:27.257
and thank you, Jenny for
asking this question.

01:01:29.180 --> 01:01:31.580
I think in the picture,

01:01:31.580 --> 01:01:34.350
it's not the person that
we are constraining.

01:01:34.350 --> 01:01:36.110
It's the representation of the person

01:01:36.110 --> 01:01:38.020
that we are constraining,

01:01:38.020 --> 01:01:40.680
and it's actually exactly
doing what you're suggesting

01:01:40.680 --> 01:01:41.920
in some sense.

01:01:41.920 --> 01:01:44.390
So the constraint is on the information

01:01:44.390 --> 01:01:46.313
that we are going to show this person.

01:01:47.480 --> 01:01:52.030
So in the most simple sense,

01:01:52.030 --> 01:01:54.853
what it translates to is that let's say,

01:01:56.100 --> 01:01:57.750
you know, Twitter cannot show you

01:01:57.750 --> 01:01:59.340
completely polarized content,

01:01:59.340 --> 01:02:02.270
it has to show you at least let's say 20%.

01:02:02.270 --> 01:02:04.720
Now this 20% is an arbitrary number,

01:02:04.720 --> 01:02:06.370
but let's say 20% content,

01:02:06.370 --> 01:02:09.640
which may differ from your
current political view.

01:02:09.640 --> 01:02:11.350
And then the question arises,

01:02:11.350 --> 01:02:13.530
how do you choose this content?

01:02:13.530 --> 01:02:17.120
Now, the real, you know,

01:02:17.120 --> 01:02:19.160
so we all know through personal experience

01:02:19.160 --> 01:02:21.450
that if you meet a random person

01:02:21.450 --> 01:02:24.910
with a diametrically
opposite political opinion,

01:02:24.910 --> 01:02:26.630
it's unlikely they will have any impact

01:02:26.630 --> 01:02:28.400
on your political opinion.

01:02:28.400 --> 01:02:30.690
So we want to pick articles,

01:02:30.690 --> 01:02:35.500
which are the most likely
to have an impact on you.

01:02:35.500 --> 01:02:37.620
So, I mean, in some sense to explain it

01:02:37.620 --> 01:02:40.410
in colloquial language,
I mean, if your friend

01:02:40.410 --> 01:02:43.550
or your family tried to
explain something to you,

01:02:43.550 --> 01:02:45.750
you might be more swayed

01:02:45.750 --> 01:02:49.020
as opposed to a person who
you don't like a priory,

01:02:49.020 --> 01:02:52.800
or who you don't trust or
who you don't know a priory,

01:02:52.800 --> 01:02:53.633
right?

01:02:53.633 --> 01:02:56.130
So that the task of
selecting the right articles

01:02:56.130 --> 01:02:59.513
or the right news content
is a very important one.

01:03:00.780 --> 01:03:03.893
And that's really where the
fine optimization goes in.

01:03:05.570 --> 01:03:07.853
- So yeah, that's what we do actually.

01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:10.513
- Great, thank you.

01:03:12.110 --> 01:03:15.980
Okay, so I guess I have this
other question actually,

01:03:15.980 --> 01:03:18.690
which was about the Rooney rule.

01:03:18.690 --> 01:03:21.310
So, you know, I'm going to stray

01:03:21.310 --> 01:03:24.690
and use my own experience
as a guide, right?

01:03:24.690 --> 01:03:26.340
So isn't it true,

01:03:26.340 --> 01:03:31.030
and I think, you know, this
is echoed more generally,

01:03:31.030 --> 01:03:34.500
isn't it true that when
you have one representative

01:03:34.500 --> 01:03:37.870
of a disadvantaged
group amongst, you know,

01:03:37.870 --> 01:03:40.000
in that interview example that you had

01:03:41.230 --> 01:03:44.120
that then they come to stand

01:03:44.120 --> 01:03:47.570
for everything that that group represents;

01:03:47.570 --> 01:03:50.000
positive and negative?

01:03:50.000 --> 01:03:55.000
So that one person could much
more easily serve as a trigger

01:03:55.990 --> 01:03:57.950
to our biases.

01:03:57.950 --> 01:03:59.340
But if you have two,

01:03:59.340 --> 01:04:01.050
then there's a little more noise, right?

01:04:01.050 --> 01:04:05.360
So, you know, you can--

01:04:05.360 --> 01:04:06.193
- And that's hundred percent correct

01:04:06.193 --> 01:04:08.620
and that's really what our results shows.

01:04:08.620 --> 01:04:12.073
So the parameter L that I
talked about is not one.

01:04:13.160 --> 01:04:15.570
So, yeah, so that's right.

01:04:15.570 --> 01:04:19.200
So I think the question that was raised

01:04:19.200 --> 01:04:22.010
in the literature was, well, you know,

01:04:22.010 --> 01:04:24.510
what can you show about this Rooney rule?

01:04:24.510 --> 01:04:28.723
And so we cannot show much
about the L equal to one case,

01:04:29.860 --> 01:04:32.360
but we can show, at least mathematically,

01:04:32.360 --> 01:04:35.070
we can show, you know,

01:04:35.070 --> 01:04:39.350
that if L is something a
little bit larger than one,

01:04:39.350 --> 01:04:42.320
then it has, yeah,

01:04:42.320 --> 01:04:45.860
so I think intuition is
a hundred percent right,

01:04:45.860 --> 01:04:48.830
that the noise kind of cancels out, right?

01:04:48.830 --> 01:04:52.810
And then you start getting
the signal of the quality,

01:04:52.810 --> 01:04:54.860
because from one sample, you know,

01:04:54.860 --> 01:04:57.070
you might get something
which is pretty far off

01:04:57.070 --> 01:05:01.160
from, you know, the
true mean, for instance.

01:05:01.160 --> 01:05:02.833
But if you have many samples,

01:05:03.820 --> 01:05:05.820
then you get a better idea of the group.

01:05:06.806 --> 01:05:09.760
So thank you for asking this question.

01:05:09.760 --> 01:05:11.920
Unfortunately, we already solved this

01:05:11.920 --> 01:05:16.229
and I can tell you more about it.

01:05:16.229 --> 01:05:17.896
- Glad to hear that.

01:05:18.850 --> 01:05:21.680
- Yeah, but you're right
on track on this one, yes.

01:05:21.680 --> 01:05:24.750
- L had to be greater than one.

01:05:24.750 --> 01:05:26.990
- It means you completely
understood what I said,

01:05:26.990 --> 01:05:29.733
which is very flattering.

01:05:30.639 --> 01:05:35.639
- So I think there's
a question from Talia.

01:05:35.710 --> 01:05:37.370
And the question is you described

01:05:37.370 --> 01:05:41.020
the context specific nature
of bias and fairness.

01:05:41.020 --> 01:05:45.770
What are the implications
of that context dependence

01:05:45.770 --> 01:05:48.730
for efforts to develop more general AI?

01:05:48.730 --> 01:05:50.500
Are there ways to reduce bias

01:05:50.500 --> 01:05:54.140
in the application of
relatively general AI tools?

01:05:54.140 --> 01:05:55.573
It's a great question, yeah.

01:05:58.170 --> 01:06:00.580
- Yeah, so this is a very good question,

01:06:00.580 --> 01:06:02.513
but a very hard question as well.

01:06:06.186 --> 01:06:09.943
I mean, if I were to give a
talk on AI and machine learning,

01:06:11.850 --> 01:06:16.850
I think I would first start
by kind of bringing it down

01:06:17.180 --> 01:06:18.650
to the level of actually is

01:06:18.650 --> 01:06:20.950
than what the media portrays it is.

01:06:20.950 --> 01:06:23.420
So we are nowhere close
to the kind of stuff.

01:06:23.420 --> 01:06:25.460
I mean, you know, there
have been successes.

01:06:25.460 --> 01:06:28.003
So I'm not worried about
right now generally.

01:06:30.750 --> 01:06:32.820
But in a very specific context,

01:06:32.820 --> 01:06:34.883
let's say you design an algorithm,

01:06:36.100 --> 01:06:38.890
which potentially can be
used in multiple contexts.

01:06:38.890 --> 01:06:41.340
Then there has to be a component there,

01:06:41.340 --> 01:06:46.150
which somehow quickly
recognizes or learns the context

01:06:46.150 --> 01:06:49.740
and then specializes itself

01:06:51.288 --> 01:06:54.720
to a well-agreed-upon notion
of fairness in that context.

01:06:54.720 --> 01:06:56.600
Now, it cannot just arbitrarily choose.

01:06:56.600 --> 01:06:57.750
But I mean, you know,

01:06:57.750 --> 01:07:01.733
so the architects of the algorithm, right,

01:07:04.275 --> 01:07:08.303
have to agree a priory,
you know, for what context,

01:07:08.303 --> 01:07:12.960
what notion of fairness
or bias is important.

01:07:12.960 --> 01:07:15.130
And so that information has to be included

01:07:15.130 --> 01:07:17.020
as a part of the algorithm itself.

01:07:17.020 --> 01:07:19.020
I don't know if it answers your question,

01:07:19.020 --> 01:07:23.813
but we are very far from
constructing any such thing.

01:07:25.020 --> 01:07:30.020
But yeah, these are the big,
big questions, the frontiers.

01:07:30.980 --> 01:07:33.063
Hopefully in the next few years,

01:07:33.930 --> 01:07:35.403
we will get to some of these.

01:07:39.490 --> 01:07:44.490
- So please go ahead and
share and type your questions.

01:07:46.810 --> 01:07:49.150
So Nisheeth, while we are
waiting for someone else,

01:07:49.150 --> 01:07:51.650
I'm gonna pipe in again.

01:07:51.650 --> 01:07:54.800
So, you know, in physics
we have this intuition

01:07:54.800 --> 01:07:59.460
that when the central limit
theorem operates, right?

01:07:59.460 --> 01:08:03.630
So when you marginalize over
many, many, many observations,

01:08:03.630 --> 01:08:07.340
you kind of get rid of the noise.

01:08:07.340 --> 01:08:11.110
I mean, would I be right in saying that

01:08:11.110 --> 01:08:14.410
that isn't quite true
in the kind of problems?

01:08:14.410 --> 01:08:16.470
- So that's a very precise
mathematical theorem.

01:08:16.470 --> 01:08:20.200
I mean, thank you for
asking that question.

01:08:20.200 --> 01:08:22.030
So what does the central
limit theorem say?

01:08:22.030 --> 01:08:24.630
It says the following: suppose
you have random variables,

01:08:24.630 --> 01:08:26.910
X1 to Xn, right?

01:08:26.910 --> 01:08:30.220
And you take the average or take the sum

01:08:30.220 --> 01:08:31.697
and divide them by square root n.

01:08:31.697 --> 01:08:33.360
Square root n is the variance, right?

01:08:33.360 --> 01:08:36.470
Let's say these are
Gaussian, I mean, whatever.

01:08:36.470 --> 01:08:37.840
These are random variables with mean zero.

01:08:37.840 --> 01:08:39.940
- They are not correlated with each other.

01:08:41.140 --> 01:08:42.210
- Yeah, not correlated,

01:08:42.210 --> 01:08:45.420
but the important point is that you took n

01:08:45.420 --> 01:08:47.520
and the function that
you computed of them,

01:08:47.520 --> 01:08:48.770
which is the sum,

01:08:48.770 --> 01:08:53.050
or rather, let's say sum
divided by some number.

01:08:53.050 --> 01:08:55.880
No particular random variable

01:08:55.880 --> 01:08:59.233
has a very high 'influence'
on this function.

01:09:00.290 --> 01:09:04.140
So the reason why physics
works is that nature

01:09:04.140 --> 01:09:06.600
I mean, very rarely we'll have functions

01:09:06.600 --> 01:09:10.690
where it is a function of
many, many variables, right?

01:09:10.690 --> 01:09:14.570
But somehow a very small set of variables

01:09:14.570 --> 01:09:19.383
dictate the outcome of the function.

01:09:21.890 --> 01:09:24.580
But you know, the kind of
algorithms we are talking about

01:09:24.580 --> 01:09:26.750
in this, we are not just doing

01:09:26.750 --> 01:09:29.070
a simple statistics calculation.

01:09:29.070 --> 01:09:32.450
We are optimizing, we are
picking the maximum among,

01:09:32.450 --> 01:09:36.210
let's say a set of K people, or K scores,

01:09:36.210 --> 01:09:40.670
or something very brittle,
I would say, you know.

01:09:40.670 --> 01:09:43.710
So there's a brittleness
that comes into it.

01:09:43.710 --> 01:09:46.252
And I think this is a perfect question

01:09:46.252 --> 01:09:49.550
for our next conversation on Monday,

01:09:49.550 --> 01:09:53.070
because I mean, the
brittleness of these algorithms

01:09:53.070 --> 01:09:55.220
is what Alexander Madre studies

01:09:56.120 --> 01:09:57.970
without worrying so much about bias,

01:09:57.970 --> 01:10:00.030
but you know, about the brittleness.

01:10:00.030 --> 01:10:03.210
And one of the ways in
which you can kind of

01:10:03.210 --> 01:10:05.770
include your intuition from physics

01:10:05.770 --> 01:10:07.810
in the design of these algorithms

01:10:07.810 --> 01:10:10.020
is to smoothen these functions out.

01:10:10.020 --> 01:10:11.140
- Exactly.

01:10:11.140 --> 01:10:12.277
I was gonna say you smoothen.

01:10:12.277 --> 01:10:14.130
- You smoothen these functions out.

01:10:14.130 --> 01:10:16.280
So what are ways to
smoothen functions out?

01:10:16.280 --> 01:10:19.130
I mean, in mathematics,
there are many ways.

01:10:19.130 --> 01:10:20.780
I mean, there are these operators,

01:10:20.780 --> 01:10:22.010
you can take a function

01:10:22.010 --> 01:10:24.780
and you want to compute
its value at one point.

01:10:24.780 --> 01:10:27.540
Well, you can take a small
neighborhood around you

01:10:27.540 --> 01:10:30.380
and just take the average
around this point.

01:10:30.380 --> 01:10:33.060
So, you know, you can
do a short random walk

01:10:33.060 --> 01:10:35.270
or Brownian emotion around this, you know,

01:10:35.270 --> 01:10:37.940
and then take the
expectation of the function.

01:10:37.940 --> 01:10:38.890
So there are various tricks

01:10:38.890 --> 01:10:41.840
in which you can smoothen
the function out.

01:10:41.840 --> 01:10:45.800
And smoothening helps carry
through your intuition.

01:10:45.800 --> 01:10:49.810
But what is not clear
is that this smoothening

01:10:49.810 --> 01:10:53.580
will not very dramatically
impact the accuracy.

01:10:53.580 --> 01:10:54.540
- Yup.

01:10:54.540 --> 01:10:57.122
- So when you talk about, you know,

01:10:57.122 --> 01:11:00.530
we're talking about trillion
dollar economies, okay,

01:11:00.530 --> 01:11:02.070
This is not a joke.

01:11:02.070 --> 01:11:06.000
A second decimal place in drop in accuracy

01:11:06.000 --> 01:11:10.720
of something means many,
many, many more dollars

01:11:10.720 --> 01:11:12.033
than I have or I will ever see, okay.

01:11:12.033 --> 01:11:13.730
- Yeah, huge consequences,
huge consequences.

01:11:13.730 --> 01:11:15.470
- Right, huge consequences.

01:11:15.470 --> 01:11:19.380
So convincing a company
such as Facebook or Google

01:11:19.380 --> 01:11:22.530
or whatever to move towards algorithms

01:11:22.530 --> 01:11:27.530
which are less brittle is
currently an impossible task.

01:11:29.370 --> 01:11:30.203
- [Priya] Right.

01:11:30.203 --> 01:11:32.230
- You know, and the only way to do that

01:11:32.230 --> 01:11:34.430
is to first formulate a regulation.

01:11:34.430 --> 01:11:38.600
I mean, you know, without any regulation

01:11:39.630 --> 01:11:42.590
which ties their hand
somehow, you cannot...

01:11:45.610 --> 01:11:47.590
I mean, this is really
the context of this talk.

01:11:47.590 --> 01:11:48.960
What is the purpose of this talk?

01:11:48.960 --> 01:11:52.090
The purpose of this talk is, you know,

01:11:52.090 --> 01:11:55.620
to give influential people
who may not be experts

01:11:55.620 --> 01:11:58.410
in algorithms, but who have the influence

01:11:58.410 --> 01:12:00.390
in various domains, right?

01:12:00.390 --> 01:12:03.130
Go and have a conversation
about algorithmic bias

01:12:03.130 --> 01:12:06.330
and the impact, and then, you know,

01:12:06.330 --> 01:12:07.990
eventually gained the ability

01:12:07.990 --> 01:12:12.010
at the highest levels of
decision-making in this country

01:12:12.010 --> 01:12:15.830
and others to have the right conversation

01:12:15.830 --> 01:12:17.370
with these tech companies,

01:12:17.370 --> 01:12:19.647
to hold and twist their arms and say,

01:12:19.647 --> 01:12:21.520
"No, you have to do this."

01:12:21.520 --> 01:12:22.930
Because if you go and say, "No, do this,"

01:12:22.930 --> 01:12:24.730
when you know it not possible to do.

01:12:26.430 --> 01:12:27.667
- Yeah, no, right.

01:12:28.540 --> 01:12:31.652
- But yeah, designing smooth algorithms

01:12:31.652 --> 01:12:36.150
is what will solve many
problems, actually,

01:12:36.150 --> 01:12:37.910
not just this problem.

01:12:37.910 --> 01:12:39.180
- To be continued on Monday.

01:12:39.180 --> 01:12:40.209
Thank you so much, Nisheeth.

